---
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title="Projects | Ravikumar Buragapu"
			description="Open-source projects in production AI, LLM infrastructure, MCP, and engineering."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<h1>Projects</h1>
			<p>
				Tools and experiments exploring how to engineer AI systems
				that are measurable, useful, and responsible in practice.
			</p>
			<p>
				My approach is straightforward: build the system, measure its
				behavior, understand its limitations, and use the evidence to
				improve engineering decisions.
			</p>

			<section aria-labelledby="fit">
				<h2 id="fit">How these fit together</h2>
				<p>
					Production AI needs three things: ways to measure how a
					system behaves, applications that show controlled autonomy
					in practice, and a first-principles understanding of the
					components underneath. The projects below are organized
					the same way. The evaluation work asks whether we can trust
					what we measure, the agentic applications explore what
					agents should be allowed to do, and the research notebook
					tests how the underlying pieces actually behave.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section aria-labelledby="evaluation">
				<h2 id="evaluation">Evaluation and benchmarking</h2>
				<p>
					Making LLM serving, retrieval, and judging measurable and
					reproducible.
				</p>

				<article class="project featured">
					<p class="tag">FEATURED · OPEN-SOURCE TOOLKIT</p>
					<h3><a href="/projects/benchmarkkit">BenchmarkKit</a></h3>
					<p>
						An open-source toolkit for measuring LLM inference and
						RAG latency, throughput, cost, retrieval recall, and
						answer support.
					</p>
					<p class="question">
						Question: how do model configurations, retrieval
						strategies, and infrastructure choices affect the
						performance and quality of AI applications, and how can
						benchmark results support architecture decisions?
					</p>
				</article>

				<article class="project featured">
					<p class="tag">FEATURED · RESEARCH STUDY</p>
					<h3><a href="/projects/rag-judge-eval">Validated RAG Eval Harness</a></h3>
					<p>
						A human-validated comparison of three LLM-as-judge prompts
						for answer correctness and citation support, with Cohen's
						kappa, question-type disagreements, and labeling costs.
					</p>
					<p class="question">
						Question: when can automated evaluation provide enough
						evidence to support decisions about AI system quality?
					</p>
				</article>

				<article class="project">
					<p class="tag">RESEARCH STUDY</p>
					<h3><a href="/projects/chunking-ablation">Chunking Strategy Ablation</a></h3>
					<p>
						A controlled comparison of fixed, recursive,
						sentence-window, and semantic chunking on retrieval,
						context tokens, answer accuracy, and latency.
					</p>
					<p class="question">
						Question: how much does chunking strategy influence
						retrieval effectiveness, and under what conditions?
					</p>
				</article>
			</section>

			<section aria-labelledby="agentic">
				<h2 id="agentic">Agentic applications</h2>
				<p>
					Applications built around multi-agent workflows, retrieval,
					and constrained tool access.
				</p>

				<article class="project featured">
					<p class="tag">FEATURED · APPLICATION · MCP · LANGGRAPH</p>
					<h3><a href="/projects/chief-of-staff">Personal Chief of Staff</a></h3>
					<p>
						A local-first, LangGraph-supervised assistant for daily
						briefings, email triage, meeting prep, and scheduling
						suggestions through read-only MCP servers.
					</p>
					<p class="question">
						Question: how can agentic applications provide useful
						automation without giving up security, transparency, and
						user control?
					</p>
				</article>

				<article class="project">
					<p class="tag">APPLICATION · RAG · LANGGRAPH</p>
					<h3><a href="/projects/research-chat">Chat with My Research</a></h3>
					<p>
						A citation-first, LangGraph RAG application that decomposes
						multi-hop questions with MCTS, compares search strategies,
						retrieves local source passages, and evaluates citations.
					</p>
					<p class="question">
						Question: how can retrieval and reasoning strategies
						improve the quality of answers to complex research
						questions?
					</p>
				</article>
			</section>

			<section aria-labelledby="notebook">
				<h2 id="notebook">Research notebook</h2>
				<p>
					From-scratch studies of the components underneath modern AI
					systems, each with a controlled comparison.
				</p>

				<article class="project">
					<p class="tag">RESEARCH STUDY · VECTOR SEARCH</p>
					<h3><a href="/projects/learned-ann">Learned ANN Index</a></h3>
					<p>
						An experimental recursive partition model for vector
						search, compared with IVF-PQ, HNSW, and ScaNN on recall,
						throughput, build time, and index memory.
					</p>
					<p class="question">Focus: retrieval accuracy, search latency, memory usage, and indexing behavior.</p>
				</article>

				<article class="project">
					<p class="tag">RESEARCH STUDY · TOKENIZATION</p>
					<h3><a href="/projects/tokenizer-ablation">Tokenizer Ablation</a></h3>
					<p>
						A byte-level BPE tokenizer trained from scratch and
						evaluated against downstream code-identifier accuracy
						under fixed token and byte budgets.
					</p>
					<p class="question">Focus: vocabulary design, sequence length, tokenization efficiency, and downstream performance.</p>
				</article>

				<article class="project">
					<p class="tag">RESEARCH STUDY · SEQUENCE MODELS</p>
					<h3><a href="/projects/sequence-models">Sequence Models at Small Scale</a></h3>
					<p>
						A parameter-matched comparison of RNN, LSTM, GRU, and a
						tiny Transformer on character prediction and 100-step recall.
					</p>
					<p class="question">Focus: architecture trade-offs, computational efficiency, training behavior, and model quality.</p>
				</article>

				<article class="project">
					<p class="tag">RESEARCH STUDY · EMBEDDINGS</p>
					<h3><a href="/projects/word2vec-lab">Word2Vec Lab</a></h3>
					<p>
						A from-scratch NumPy skip-gram study with a 27-setting
						hyperparameter sweep, analogy and neighbor evaluation,
						and optional GloVe, FastText, and t-SNE comparisons.
					</p>
					<p class="question">Focus: representation quality, training objectives, embedding geometry, and evaluation.</p>
				</article>
			</section>

			<section aria-labelledby="philosophy">
				<h2 id="philosophy">Research philosophy</h2>
				<p>
					I approach AI engineering through experimentation and
					evidence. A successful experiment is not necessarily one
					that produces the best metric. It is one that improves
					understanding, exposes trade-offs, and supports better
					engineering decisions.
				</p>
				<p>
					I'm especially interested in how model capabilities,
					evaluation methods, infrastructure behavior, and operational
					reliability relate to one another.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section aria-labelledby="next">
				<h2 id="next">Related</h2>
				<p>
					<a href="/writing">Read the writing behind these projects</a>
					·
					<a href="/speak">Speaking</a>
					·
					<a href="/about">About me</a>
				</p>
			</section>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.project {
		padding: 1rem 0;
		border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
	}
	.project h3 {
		margin: 0 0 0.25em 0;
	}
	.project p {
		margin: 0.4rem 0;
	}
	.tag {
		font-size: 0.75em;
		letter-spacing: 0.06em;
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
	}
	.question {
		font-size: 0.9em;
		font-style: italic;
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
	}
	.featured {
		border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
		padding-left: 1rem;
	}
</style>